note right of d5
  **The mirror rule (§4.6.3 rule 2).** A `trading` rail card is also a `dev_board`
  rail card unless the module says otherwise: the **same** `factory` value appears
  in two instances that differ only in `surface_id`. There is never a "Trading
  version" and a "Dev Board version" of a widget. The reverse is not true — Dev
  Board additionally holds what Trading does not.

  **When in doubt, start on Dev Board:** an unproven card is created with
  `surface_id = "dev_board"` first, and promoted by changing that one slot value.
  That is what ADR D4 means in practice.
end note

note bottom of b2
  **Every screen is a surface (§4.6.3 rule 3).** Because `backtest` declares
  `accepts = {RAIL, MODAL}`, `strategy` and `market_data` can place instances on it
  **without `backtesting` importing either module**.
end note

note bottom of w1
  `welcome` and `settings` belong to the **shell**, not to a module: they are
  surfaces about the application itself.

  A guard renders the *UI map* — the set of these instances keyed by
  (surface_id, place) — and fails on a factory contributed nowhere, or on a
  `place` literal that does not exist.
end note
